            <ArticleTitle>Multislice computed tomography in the diagnosis of coronary anomalies with superimposed coronary artery disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>351-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2459/JCM.0b013e3283403984</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Multislice computed tomography (MSCT) is widely used to complement conventional angiography in the diagnosis and characterisation of coronary artery anomalies. MSCT adds relevant 3-dimensional information that relates the course of anomalous vessels to specific anatomical structures of the heart. The utility of MSCT in patients with coronary anomalies is exemplified in a patient with superimposed coronary artery disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
